 I read gamenotes (it has no .txt extension), today's ledger journal-2026-09-20.json, and your five screenshots. I changed nothing. The causes below come from your data and notes, not from
  reading the code, so each one needs checking before we build on it.

  What the data says

  1. Food is a yearly cycle, and it is the root cause of most of the problems.
  - Every year the pattern is the same. Dec and Jan have 180 fed and 0 unfed. Feb is partial (81/99 or 90/90). From Mar to Nov it is 30 fed and 150 unfed.
  - The harvest arrives once a year, and the hub, market and houses drain it in one to two months. That leaves about 9 months of famine.
  - The constant 30 fed is 15 houses × 2 foragers each, which is the subsistence you described. It is the only steady food source.
  - Population never passes 180 because 15 houses × 12 is the masure cap. Houses evolve to "logis" in Dec, then drop back to masure in Feb once the famine hits.

  2. The global totals in the traceability panel are meaningless. 10629 / 7885 / 2744 is a sum of monthly snapshots. Each month adds about 180 people again, so it is neither a population nor
  a distinct count. The per-year totals (e.g. 1089) have the same flaw. The monthly cards are the useful part.

  3. The economy is dominated by unemployment benefit. Over the session:

  ┌──────────────────────┬─────────┐
  │     Ledger line      │ Amount  │
  ├──────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ Unemployment benefit │ 514,570 │
  ├──────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ Salaries             │ 74,000  │
  ├──────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ Payroll tax          │ 67,437  │
  ├──────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ Citizen tax          │ 17,850  │
  └──────────────────────┴─────────┘

  The balance goes from +174 around turn 100 to -509,004 at turn 396. About 140 of the 180 residents are unemployed and paid 70% of a salary.

  4. Several numbers on screen contradict each other.
  - The house panel shows "2/12 nourriture" while every stock is 0.
  - It shows 12 for a house of 13 people.
  - Your notes report 174 pop with 144 famished.

  Proposals

  A. Quick fixes (small, safe)
  1. Remove the global "Vue Globale" block and the per-year totals from the traceability panel. Replace them with a peak / average population per year and the number of months fully fed.
     Those numbers show the famine cycle directly.
  2. Add the level next to the house name, e.g. "Masure (1)".
  3. In the info panel, show one coherent food line (stock / need / days covered) and drop the ambiguous "x/12".
  4. When a workplace isn't staffed, show why: "no residents of that skill" versus "not enough population".
  5. Put the services in tier order in the toolbar (faith, then market).

  B. Food flow (the main gameplay fix)
  1. Run the supply-balance calculator we already planned. It should give farms needed per year and the reserve needed to bridge Dec to Nov. Right now the numbers can't work: 5 farms for 180
     people leave 9 months uncovered.
  2. Distribute by need, not all at once. Each market would release only what its houses need per month. The hub would then hold the real stock, as in Caesar 3 (your note on line 46). The
     market would also show the unmet demand.
  3. Give houses a real food buffer. At the moment they hold about 1 month, so the yearly harvest cannot be spread over the year.
  4. Balance farm yield against storage capacity. The silo and market maxStock values (1000 and 500) should cover a year of demand for the population the city is meant to reach. This is
     catalog-only.
  5. Replace forager subsistence with the quality-food idea from your notes. Foraging would be a low-quality fallback. Better food (fruit, game, bread) would count for evolution and diet
     variety.

  C. Evolution stability
  - Use hysteresis. A house would evolve as soon as the requirements are met, but devolve only after N consecutive months unfed. One bad month currently resets a whole tier, and the Dec/Feb
    flip-flop shows it.
  - Show how many places are left before the population cap, as in Caesar 3.
  
  D. Labor and money
  - Look at why 85% are unemployed while the hub shows 0/4 workers yet still works. There is probably a bug in how the hub is staffed, or its "operational" status is wrong.
  - Change the unemployment benefit early on. Options: none, 20 to 30%, or a rate that only applies above a threshold. Otherwise no scenario can ever balance. You said money comes later, so
    I'd only flag it for now.
  - Add a per-house-type tab showing which buildings its residents can staff and how many. Greyed-out entries would mean the building doesn't exist yet in the city. That covers your "global"
    note and helps players plan where to build.

  E. Later, on the presentation side
  - A colour intensity on the no-food icon (green to red) per house.
  - Visible harvest and delivery walkers between farm, hub and market.
  - Roads with pavements and a road connection to the outside for commerce.
  - Keep the tool panel open while placing buildings.

  Suggested order

  1. A1 and B1 first: the panel cleanup, plus the calculator so we know the real targets.
  2. B2/B3/B4 for the food flow, then C.
  3. D after that, since it depends on the food rebalance.

  Which of these should I start on, and do you want the cleanup (A1) and the calculator (B1) done together?

